# Good vs bad examples

Companion to `.cursor/rules/engineering-conventions.mdc` (the rules). The examples use a fictional `shows` feature (selector prefix `st-`, containers = smart components, presentational = dumb components). If an example conflicts with the rules or `.cursorrules`, the rules win.

**Do not read this whole file.** Read only the sections for your task (search for the section title):
- developer: the sections for the topics you touch (state/resource 8, templates 9, inputs/outputs 10, forms 11, DI 12, change detection 13, routing 14, spartan/Tailwind 15, JSDoc 16, ports/adapters 1-2, component folders 19).
- reviewer: only a section you need to settle a doubtful pattern.
- tester: section 17 (tests), plus 2 and 4 for what to fake.
- architect: sections 1-4, 6-7, 14, 18.

---

## 1. Single responsibility (SOLID-S): split fetching, rules and rendering

Bad: one component fetches, maps, applies business rules and renders.

```ts
@Component({ selector: 'st-shows', template: `...` })
export class ShowsComponent {
  private http = inject(HttpClient);
  shows: any[] = [];
  ngOnInit() {
    this.http.get<any[]>('/api/shows').subscribe((r) => {
      this.shows = r.filter((s) => new Date(s.startsAt) > new Date()).map((s) => ({ ...s, price: s.cents / 100 }));
    });
  }
}
```

Good: container wires use case to a presentational component; mapping/rules live in domain/application/adapter.

```ts
/** Smart component: connects the use case to the UI. */
@Component({
  selector: 'st-shows-page',
  changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush,
  imports: [ShowListComponent],
  template: `
    @if (shows.hasValue()) {
      <st-show-list [shows]="shows.value()" />
    } @else if (shows.error()) {
      <p>Could not load shows.</p>
    } @else {
      <p>Loading...</p>
    }
  `,
})
export class ShowsPageContainer {
  private readonly listUpcomingShows = inject(ListUpcomingShows);
  protected readonly shows = resource({ loader: ({ abortSignal }) => this.listUpcomingShows.execute(abortSignal) });
}
```

## 2. Dependency inversion (SOLID-D) + Hexagonal: domain/application never import Angular HTTP

Bad: use case/domain depends on the concrete adapter.

```ts
@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })
export class ListUpcomingShows {
  private http = inject(HttpClient); // application layer knows about HTTP
  execute() { return this.http.get<Show[]>('/api/shows'); }
}
```

Good: the application layer owns a port; an adapter implements it; wiring happens in a `provideX()`.

```ts
// application layer (no Angular HTTP imports)
/** Output port: source of shows. Implementations must return only valid `Show`s and reject on transport errors. */
export abstract class ShowsRepository {
  abstract findAll(signal?: AbortSignal): Promise<readonly Show[]>;
}

@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })
export class ListUpcomingShows {
  private readonly shows = inject(ShowsRepository);
  async execute(signal?: AbortSignal): Promise<readonly Show[]> {
    const all = await this.shows.findAll(signal);
    return all.filter((s) => s.isUpcoming(new Date()));
  }
}

// adapter layer
/** HTTP adapter for {@link ShowsRepository}. */
@Injectable()
export class HttpShowsRepository extends ShowsRepository {
  private readonly http = inject(HttpClient);
  override async findAll(signal?: AbortSignal) {
    const dtos = await firstValueFrom(this.http.get<ShowDto[]>('/api/shows'));
    return dtos.map(toShow);
  }
}

export function provideShowsHttp(): Provider[] {
  return [{ provide: ShowsRepository, useClass: HttpShowsRepository }];
}
```

## 3. Open/closed (SOLID-O): extend by adding implementations, not by editing conditionals

Bad: every new payment method edits the same function.

```ts
function fee(method: string, amount: number) {
  if (method === 'card') return amount * 0.02;
  if (method === 'paypal') return amount * 0.03 + 0.3;
  return 0; // edit here for each new method
}
```

Good: a small port plus one class per variant; a registry picks the implementation.

```ts
export interface FeePolicy { readonly method: string; feeFor(amount: number): number }
export const FEE_POLICIES = new InjectionToken<readonly FeePolicy[]>('FEE_POLICIES');
// new method = new FeePolicy + one provider line, existing code unchanged
```

(Apply only when variants actually exist or are required now; otherwise it is YAGNI, see section 8.)

## 4. Interface segregation (SOLID-I) and Liskov (SOLID-L)

Bad: fat port; an implementation breaks the contract.

```ts
export abstract class ShowsRepository {
  abstract findAll(): Promise<Show[]>;
  abstract save(show: Show): Promise<void>;
  abstract delete(id: string): Promise<void>;
  abstract exportCsv(): Promise<Blob>;
}
export class ReadOnlyShowsRepository extends ShowsRepository {
  override save() { throw new Error('not supported'); } // violates the contract
  // ...
}
```

Good: small ports; each consumer depends only on what it uses.

```ts
export abstract class ShowsReader { abstract findAll(): Promise<readonly Show[]>; }
export abstract class ShowsWriter { abstract save(show: Show): Promise<void>; }
```

## 5. KISS: the simplest thing that satisfies the scenario

Bad: generic machinery for a single filter.

```ts
class FilterEngine<T, K extends keyof T> {
  constructor(private strategies: Map<K, (v: T[K]) => boolean>) {}
  apply(items: T[]) { /* ... */ }
}
```

Good:

```ts
protected readonly visible = computed(() => this.shows().filter((s) => s.title.toLowerCase().includes(this.query().toLowerCase())));
```

## 6. DRY: share knowledge, not coincidence

Bad: the same rule copied in the form validator, the use case and a pipe.

```ts
// form:   price > 0 && price <= 10000
// usecase: if (p <= 0 || p > 10000) throw ...
// pipe:   p > 10000 ? 'invalid' : ...
```

Good: one domain definition, reused.

```ts
/** Ticket price in cents. Invariant: 1..1_000_000. */
export class Price {
  static readonly MAX = 1_000_000;
  private constructor(readonly cents: number) {}
  static of(cents: number): Price {
    if (!Number.isInteger(cents) || cents < 1 || cents > Price.MAX) throw new RangeError('Invalid price');
    return new Price(cents);
  }
}
```

Do not merge code that merely looks alike but changes for different reasons (for example two screens with similar markup but different business rules); extract on the third real repetition.

## 7. YAGNI: build only what the scenarios require

Bad: speculative options and layers.

```ts
export interface ShowListOptions { pageSize?: number; sortBy?: string; groupBy?: string; virtualScroll?: boolean; theme?: 'a' | 'b' }
export abstract class AbstractShowListFactory { /* "we may need other lists later" */ }
```

Good: only what the current WHEN/THEN scenarios use.

```ts
readonly shows = input.required<readonly Show[]>();
```

## 8. State: Signals, `computed`, `resource` (no subscribe-and-assign, no toSignal/async pipe)

Bad:

```ts
export class UserCard implements OnInit, OnDestroy {
  user$ = new BehaviorSubject<User | null>(null);
  private sub = new Subscription();
  constructor(private api: UserApi) {}
  ngOnInit() { this.sub.add(this.api.get().subscribe((u) => this.user$.next(u))); }
  ngOnDestroy() { this.sub.unsubscribe(); }
}
// template: {{ (user$ | async)?.name }}
```

Also bad: `user = toSignal(this.api.get$())` (forbidden).

Good:

```ts
export class UserCard {
  private readonly userId = input.required<string>();
  private readonly api = inject(UserApi);
  protected readonly user = resource({
    params: () => ({ id: this.userId() }),
    loader: ({ params, abortSignal }) => this.api.get(params.id, abortSignal),
  });
  protected readonly name = computed(() => (this.user.hasValue() ? this.user.value().name : ''));
}
```

Never read `resource.value()` unguarded in a template or `computed`: it throws in the `error` status (use `hasValue()` / `error()`). Use `httpResource` for plain HTTP reads. Use RxJS only for complex event streams; a debounced search field needs no RxJS at all with Signal Forms:

```ts
protected readonly searchForm = form(signal({ query: '' }), (p) => {
  debounce(p.query, 300); // delays model updates; a resource keyed on the model re-fetches
});
```

## 9. Templates: control flow, `@let`, stable `track`

Bad:

```html
<li *ngFor="let s of shows; let i = index; trackBy: byIndex" *ngIf="s.visible">{{ s.title | async }}</li>
<div *ngIf="(user$ | async) as u">{{ u.name }}</div>
```

Good:

```html
@for (show of shows(); track show.id) {
  @let title = show.title.toUpperCase();
  <li>{{ title }}</li>
} @empty {
  <li>No shows</li>
}
@if (user.hasValue()) {
  @let u = user.value();
  <p>{{ u.name }}</p>
}
```

A template longer than 5 lines lives entirely in the component folder as `<component>.html` (`templateUrl: './status-badge.html'`). Five lines or fewer may stay in `template`. Do not keep part of the markup inline and part in the file.

## 10. Inputs and outputs

Bad:

```ts
@Input() show!: Show;
@Output() selected = new EventEmitter<Show>();
ngOnChanges() { this.label = this.show.title.toUpperCase(); }
```

Good:

```ts
/** Show to render. */
readonly show = input.required<Show>();
/** Emits when the user picks the show. */
readonly selected = output<Show>();
protected readonly label = computed(() => this.show().title.toUpperCase());
```

Two-way state: `model()`; derived writable state that resets with inputs: `linkedSignal`.

## 11. Signal Forms (no reactive forms, no ngModel)

Bad:

```ts
form = new FormGroup({ email: new FormControl('') });
// <input [formControl]="form.controls.email" />  or  <input [(ngModel)]="email" />
```

Good (model first, never `null`/`undefined` initial values; `submit` callback is `async`):

```ts
import { email, form, FormField, required, submit } from '@angular/forms/signals';

@Component({ imports: [FormField], template: `
  <form (submit)="onSubmit(); $event.preventDefault()">
    <input type="email" [formField]="loginForm.email" />
    @if (loginForm.email().touched() && loginForm.email().errors().length) {
      <span>{{ loginForm.email().errors()[0].message }}</span>
    }
    <button [disabled]="loginForm().invalid()">Sign in</button>
  </form>` })
export class LoginFormComponent {
  protected readonly model = signal({ email: '', remember: false });
  protected readonly loginForm = form(this.model, (p) => {
    required(p.email, { message: 'Email is required' });
    email(p.email, { message: 'Invalid email' });
  });
  protected onSubmit() {
    submit(this.loginForm, async () => { await this.login.execute(this.model()); });
  }
}
```

Remember: call a field to read its state (`form.email().valid()`); update values through the model signal; do not set `min`/`max`/`value`/`disabled`/`readonly` attributes next to `[formField]` (use schema rules).

## 12. DI and teardown

Bad:

```ts
constructor(private route: ActivatedRoute, private store: Store) {}
```

Good:

```ts
private readonly route = inject(ActivatedRoute);
private readonly analytics = inject(Analytics);

constructor() {
  inject(DestroyRef).onDestroy(() => this.analytics.flush());
}
```

For side effects that depend on signals use `effect()` (not for deriving state: use `computed`). Never mutate a signal inside `computed`.

## 13. Change detection and immutability

Bad: default change detection and in-place mutation.

```ts
@Component({ /* no changeDetection */ })
class List { items = input.required<Item[]>(); add(i: Item) { this.items().push(i); } }
```

Good:

```ts
@Component({ changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush })
class List { private readonly state = signal<readonly Item[]>([]); add(i: Item) { this.state.update((xs) => [...xs, i]); } }
```

## 14. Routing and lazy loading

Bad: `loadChildren` with NgModule, eager heavy widget, providers on the component.

Good:

```ts
export const routes: Routes = [
  {
    path: 'shows',
    loadComponent: () => import('./shows-page.container').then((m) => m.ShowsPageContainer),
    providers: [provideShowsHttp()], // created per navigation, destroyed on exit
  },
];
```

```html
@defer (on viewport) {
  <st-revenue-chart [data]="data()" />
} @placeholder {
  <div class="h-64 animate-pulse rounded-md bg-muted"></div>
} @loading (minimum 200ms) {
  <p>Loading chart...</p>
}
```

## 15. spartan-ui and Tailwind: `@shared/spartan-ui`, inline classes, `hlm()`

Bad:

```ts
import { HlmButton } from '@spartan-ng/helm/button'; // forbidden: import via @shared/spartan-ui
```

```html
<button [ngClass]="'btn ' + (active ? 'btn-active' : '')" style="color: red">Save</button>
```

and custom CSS classes in a `.scss` file for colors/spacing.

Good:

```ts
import { HlmButtonImports, hlm } from '@shared/spartan-ui';

@Component({
  imports: [HlmButtonImports],
  template: `<button hlmBtn [class]="classes()">Save</button>`,
})
export class SaveButtonComponent {
  readonly active = input(false);
  protected readonly classes = computed(() => hlm('px-4', this.active() && 'ring-2 ring-primary'));
}
```

## 16. JSDoc: explain why and the contract, not the type

Bad:

```ts
/** Gets shows. @param {string} id the id @returns {Promise<Show>} a show */
get(id: string) {}
```

Good:

```ts
/**
 * Loads a single show.
 * @param id - Show identifier.
 * @param signal - Aborts the request when the caller navigates away.
 * @returns The show, or `undefined` when it does not exist.
 * @throws {@link TransportError} when the backend is unreachable.
 */
get(id: string, signal?: AbortSignal): Promise<Show | undefined>
```

## 17. Tests: behavior, HTTP boundary, scenario names

Bad:

```ts
it('works', () => {
  const f = TestBed.createComponent(ShowsPageContainer);
  f.detectChanges();
  expect(f.componentInstance.loading).toBe(true);
  expect(f.nativeElement.innerHTML).toContain('Loading');
});
```

Good (component test through a harness, HTTP mocked at the boundary, zoneless, name from the OpenSpec scenario):

```ts
class ShowListHarness extends ComponentHarness {
  static hostSelector = 'st-show-list';
  private readonly items = this.locatorForAll('li');
  async titles() { return Promise.all((await this.items()).map((i) => i.text())); }
}

describe('Requirement: list upcoming shows', () => {
  let http: HttpTestingController;
  beforeEach(async () => {
    await TestBed.configureTestingModule({
      imports: [ShowsPageContainer],
      providers: [provideZonelessChangeDetection(), provideHttpClient(), provideHttpClientTesting(), provideShowsHttp()],
    }).compileComponents();
    http = TestBed.inject(HttpTestingController);
  });
  afterEach(() => http.verify());

  it('Scenario: upcoming shows exist - lists them by title', async () => {
    const fixture = TestBed.createComponent(ShowsPageContainer);
    const loader = TestbedHarnessEnvironment.loader(fixture);
    await fixture.whenStable();
    http.expectOne('/api/shows').flush([{ id: '1', title: 'Hamlet', startsAt: '2999-01-01T00:00:00Z', cents: 1000 }]);
    await fixture.whenStable();
    expect(await (await loader.getHarness(ShowListHarness)).titles()).toEqual(['Hamlet']);
  });
});
```

Domain/use-case tests need no TestBed: build the use case with an in-memory fake of the port.

## 18. Error handling at boundaries

Bad: swallow errors or leak transport details into the domain.

```ts
try { await this.repo.findAll(); } catch { /* ignore */ }
```

Good: translate adapter errors to domain/application errors, show a recoverable state in the UI (`resource.error()`), and never expose raw HTTP responses outside the adapter.

## 19. One folder per component

Bad: several components dropped next to each other in `src/lib`.

```text
libs/shared/ui/src/lib/status-badge.ts
libs/shared/ui/src/lib/confirm-dialog.ts
libs/shared/ui/src/lib/query-state.ts
```

Good: one folder per component, named like the file. Spec and the page UI store stay inside that folder. The barrel imports the component from the folder.

```text
libs/shared/ui/src/lib/status-badge/status-badge.ts
libs/shared/ui/src/lib/status-badge/status-badge.spec.ts
libs/users/feature/src/lib/shows-page/shows-page.ts
libs/users/feature/src/lib/shows-page/shows-page.store.ts
libs/users/feature/src/lib/shows-page/shows-page.spec.ts
```
